"""Install the control-to-predictor audit in the existing main notebook."""
from pathlib import Path
import nbformat
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[2]
TAG='control-predictors-20261029'
def install():
    p=ROOT/'synthetic_experiments/notebooks/synthetic_predictor_validation.ipynb'
    nb=nbformat.read(p,4);nb.cells=[c for c in nb.cells if TAG not in c.metadata.get('tags',[])]
    for c in nb.cells:
        if c.cell_type=='markdown' and c.source.startswith('## 5.'):
            c.source='''## 5. Scope of the current predictor audit

The following sections rebuild the **current real-model ten descriptors**, plus the Manning timing axis, across the completed grid. This is a mechanism audit, **not a fitted prediction benchmark**. It supersedes the earlier proposal to reuse the older omega-based ten-variable list for this step. The two predictor inventories are different.

A later prediction benchmark still needs grouped validation with all methods/treatments of a geometry kept together. No old R² score is presented as validation of this new population.'''
    cells=[
        nbformat.v4.new_markdown_cell("## 6. Step 1 — Do network changes affect centroid and peak shift?\n\n**SYN-CONTROL-PREDICTORS-2026-10-29.** Start with the responses only. Which of the eight controls matters, how does the response change, and does one control's effect depend on another?\n\nChoose the method and collapse type below; the same selection carries into Step 2. Centroid remains primary, peak is the comparison. No fitting or routing is performed.\n\nThe first plot shows **total sensitivity**, including interactions. Totals overlap and must not be summed. The next plot separates the strongest main and pair effects; each percentage is a nonnegative variance contribution, not a direction. All eight controls and 28 pairs remain in the exported tables.\n\nThe signed endpoint table gives direction in minutes (highest listed level minus lowest), holding the other seven controls fixed. The curves show the selected interaction, averaging the other six controls uniformly. They do not claim every individual context behaves identically.\n\nThe ranked bars share a percentage axis for centroid and peak, print exact values and scale to the displayed maximum. This replaces the fixed 0–100 colour scale that obscured smaller effects. Axis limits can differ between figures; compare the printed percentages."),
        nbformat.v4.new_code_cell("sys.path.insert(0,str(ROOT/'synthetic_experiments/investigations'))\nimport control_predictor_screen as cp\nimport hashlib\nCONTROL_METHOD = 'variable_manning'  # fixed_based / fixed_manning / variable_manning\nCONTROL_TREATMENT = 'nested/full'  # see cp.TREATMENTS\ncontrol_path=cp.OUT\nprovenance=json.loads((control_path/'provenance.json').read_text())\nassert provenance['source_response_sha256']==hashlib.sha256((cp.OUTPUT/'responses.csv').read_bytes()).hexdigest()\nfor path,digest in provenance['source_files'].items():\n    assert hashlib.sha256(Path(path).read_bytes()).hexdigest()==digest, f'Changed analysis source: {path}; rebuild control_predictor_screen.py'\ncontrol_stats=pd.read_csv(control_path/'matched_controls.csv')\nmechanisms=pd.read_csv(control_path/'factorial_signatures.csv')\ncoverage=pd.read_csv(control_path/'coverage.csv')\n\nCONTROL_X='length_multiplier'\nCONTROL_COLOUR='inner_fraction'\nPREDICTOR='delta_G_inverse_depth'  # preferred descriptor; delta_G_manning is an optional reference\ncontrol_features=pd.read_parquet(control_path/'features.parquet')\ng=control_features[(control_features.method==CONTROL_METHOD)&(control_features.child_state==CONTROL_TREATMENT)]\nselected_mechanisms=mechanisms[(mechanisms.method==CONTROL_METHOD)&(mechanisms.child_state==CONTROL_TREATMENT)]\n\ndef mechanism_heatmap(variables, terms, value, title):\n    data=selected_mechanisms.pivot(index='term',columns='variable',values=value).reindex(index=terms,columns=variables)\n    fig,ax=plt.subplots(figsize=(max(7,len(variables)*1.05),max(4,len(terms)*.4)),constrained_layout=True)\n    colors=plt.get_cmap('viridis').copy();colors.set_bad('#ddd')\n    im=ax.imshow(data,aspect='auto',vmin=0,vmax=100,cmap=colors)\n    ax.set(xticks=range(len(variables)),xticklabels=[RESPONSES.get(v,cp.LABELS.get(v,v)) for v in variables],\n           yticks=range(len(terms)),yticklabels=[' × '.join(FACTOR_LABELS.get(t,t) for t in term.split(' × ')) for term in terms],title=title+' · '+CONTROL_TREATMENT)\n    plt.setp(ax.get_xticklabels(),rotation=35,ha='right')\n    fig.colorbar(im,ax=ax,label='% of each variable’s grid variance')\n    display_figure(fig);plt.close(fig)\n\ndef control_curve(variable):\n    fig,ax=plt.subplots(figsize=(8,4),constrained_layout=True)\n    for level in manifest['config']['grid'][CONTROL_COLOUR]:\n        means=g[g[CONTROL_COLOUR]==level].groupby(CONTROL_X)[variable].mean().reindex(manifest['config']['grid'][CONTROL_X])\n        ax.plot(means.index,means.values,'o-',label=str(level))\n    ax.set(xlabel=FACTOR_LABELS[CONTROL_X],ylabel=RESPONSES.get(variable,cp.LABELS.get(variable,variable)),title=CONTROL_TREATMENT)\n    ax.legend(title=FACTOR_LABELS[CONTROL_COLOUR]);ax.grid(alpha=.15)\n    display_figure(fig);plt.close(fig)\nprint(CONTROL_METHOD, CONTROL_TREATMENT)\n\ndef response_effect_bars(terms,value,title):\n    data=selected_mechanisms.pivot(index='term',columns='variable',values=value).reindex(index=terms,columns=list(RESPONSES))\n    data=data.loc[data.max(axis=1).sort_values(ascending=False).index]\n    fig,ax=plt.subplots(figsize=(11,max(4,len(data)*.48)),constrained_layout=True)\n    y=np.arange(len(data));finite=data.to_numpy()[np.isfinite(data.to_numpy())]\n    bound=max(float(finite.max()) if len(finite) else 0.,1.)\n    for i,(response,label) in enumerate(RESPONSES.items()):\n        values=data[response].to_numpy();yy=y+(i-.5)*.34\n        ax.barh(yy,values,height=.32,label=label.replace(' (min)',''))\n        for pos,v in zip(yy,values):\n            if np.isfinite(v):ax.text(v+bound*.012,pos,f'{v:.1f}%',va='center',fontsize=8)\n    ax.set(yticks=y,yticklabels=[' × '.join(FACTOR_LABELS.get(t,t) for t in term.split(' × ')) for term in data.index],\n           xlabel='Total sensitivity (%) — overlapping contributions' if value=='total_pct' else 'Contribution to response variance (%)',\n           title=title+' · '+CONTROL_TREATMENT,xlim=(0,bound*1.18))\n    if not len(finite):ax.text(.5,.5,'Response variation below resolution screen',transform=ax.transAxes,ha='center')\n    ax.invert_yaxis();ax.grid(axis='x',alpha=.15);ax.legend();display_figure(fig);plt.close(fig)\n"),
        nbformat.v4.new_code_cell("response_effect_bars(list(FACTOR_LABELS),'total_pct','Step 1: which controls affect the responses?')\nresponse_terms=selected_mechanisms[selected_mechanisms.variable.isin(RESPONSES)].pivot(index='term',columns='variable',values='variance_pct')\nIMPORTANT_TERMS=response_terms.max(axis=1).sort_values(ascending=False).head(12).index.tolist()\nresponse_effect_bars(IMPORTANT_TERMS,'variance_pct','Step 1: strongest main effects and interactions')\ncontrasts=pd.read_csv(ANALYSIS/'signed_contrasts.csv')\ndisplay(contrasts[(contrasts.method==CONTROL_METHOD)&(contrasts.child_state==CONTROL_TREATMENT)][['response','factor','from_level','to_level','median_minutes','p10_minutes','p90_minutes']])\nfor response in RESPONSES:\n    control_curve(response)\n"),
        nbformat.v4.new_markdown_cell("### Overall effects across collapse types\n\nThe plots above describe the selected treatment. Below, **each resolved treatment receives equal weight**: its within-treatment variance contributions are calculated first, then averaged. This answers which controls generally matter across collapse types without mixing differences between their mean responses into the effects.\n\n`OVERALL_WEIGHTING='response_variance'` instead weights treatments by their response variance, answering which controls account for most of the absolute within-treatment variation. These are distinct summaries; neither mixes hydraulic methods. The coverage table shows which treatments contribute. Near-zero unresolved treatments are excluded, not counted as zero sensitivity. Total sensitivities overlap, so do not sum their bars.\n\nThe type-specific plots remain available through `CONTROL_TREATMENT` above. The overall figures are summaries of those analyses, not another pooled factorial experiment."),
        nbformat.v4.new_code_cell("OVERALL_WEIGHTING='equal_treatment'  # or 'response_variance'\nall_effects=mechanisms[(mechanisms.method==CONTROL_METHOD)&mechanisms.variable.isin(RESPONSES)].copy()\nresolved_coverage=coverage[(coverage.method==CONTROL_METHOD)&coverage.variable.isin(RESPONSES)][['child_state','variable','resolved']]\ndisplay(resolved_coverage.pivot(index='child_state',columns='variable',values='resolved'))\nparts=pd.read_csv(ANALYSIS/'variance_partition.csv').rename(columns={'response':'variable'})\nweights=parts[parts.method==CONTROL_METHOD][['child_state','variable','variance_minutes_squared']]\nall_effects=all_effects.merge(weights,on=['child_state','variable'],validate='many_to_one')\nrecords=[]\nfor (variable,term,order),group in all_effects.groupby(['variable','term','order']):\n    row=dict(variable=variable,term=term,order=order)\n    for value in ['variance_pct','total_pct']:\n        valid=group[value].notna()\n        w=np.ones(valid.sum()) if OVERALL_WEIGHTING=='equal_treatment' else group.loc[valid,'variance_minutes_squared'].to_numpy()\n        if OVERALL_WEIGHTING not in ['equal_treatment','response_variance']:raise ValueError(OVERALL_WEIGHTING)\n        row[value]=np.average(group.loc[valid,value],weights=w) if valid.any() else np.nan\n    records.append(row)\noverall_effects=pd.DataFrame(records)\nsaved_selection,saved_treatment=selected_mechanisms,CONTROL_TREATMENT\ntry:\n    selected_mechanisms=overall_effects\n    CONTROL_TREATMENT='all resolved types · '+OVERALL_WEIGHTING.replace('_',' ')\n    response_effect_bars(list(FACTOR_LABELS),'total_pct','Overall control sensitivity')\n    ranked=overall_effects.pivot(index='term',columns='variable',values='variance_pct').max(axis=1).sort_values(ascending=False).head(12).index.tolist()\n    response_effect_bars(ranked,'variance_pct','Overall main effects and interactions')\nfinally:\n    selected_mechanisms,CONTROL_TREATMENT=saved_selection,saved_treatment\n"),
        nbformat.v4.new_markdown_cell('## 7. Step 2 — Controls and interactions versus the ten current predictors\n\nThis section has three matrices answering the agreed question. Rows are experimental controls (or control pairs); columns are **exactly the ten current real-model predictors**. The extra Manning ΔG diagnostic is excluded here. No coverage-count figure or fitted model is used.\n\nChoose the selected collapse type or an overall summary below. Hydraulic methods remain separate. Compare these predictor patterns with the corresponding response patterns in Section 6.\n\n### 7a. Association and direction: eight controls × ten predictors\n\nDefault: **Spearman correlation over all grid configurations within each collapse type**. These are ordinary within-type correlations, not the previous median of matched-sweep correlations. Positive means the predictor tends to increase as the control increases; negative means it tends to decrease. Magnitude near one indicates strong monotonic association, **not a large physical effect**. Pearson is an optional linear-association view; it also does not measure absolute effect size.\n\nInternal location is ordered upstream → central → downstream; interpret its correlation as an ordered trend, not arbitrary category numbering. Undefined predictor values are excluded pairwise; the optional availability table shows the resulting population. Such correlations can describe only a restricted grid. Near-roundoff differences are rounded at a relative scale of 1e-10 before correlation.\n\nOverall mode averages the per-type correlations equally; it does not correlate a mixture of different types. Opposite directions can cancel. Use the optional per-type table to check this. Gray means undefined or constant, not zero association.'),
        nbformat.v4.new_code_cell("import predictor_association_matrices as pam\nASSOCIATION_SCOPE='selected_type'  # 'selected_type' or 'overall'\nCORRELATION='spearman'  # or 'pearson'\nSHOW_MATRIX_DETAILS=False\nCURRENT_PREDICTORS=json.loads(cp.SETTINGS.read_text())['depth_informed_10']['columns']\nassert len(CURRENT_PREDICTORS)==10\nmatrix_treatment=CONTROL_TREATMENT if ASSOCIATION_SCOPE=='selected_type' else None\nif ASSOCIATION_SCOPE not in ['selected_type','overall']:raise ValueError(ASSOCIATION_SCOPE)\nassociation_matrices,correlation_details=pam.matrices(control_features,mechanisms,manifest['config']['grid'],CURRENT_PREDICTORS,CONTROL_METHOD,matrix_treatment,CORRELATION)\nmatrix_scope=CONTROL_TREATMENT if matrix_treatment else 'equal-weight mean across available types'\nprint(CONTROL_METHOD,'·',matrix_scope)\nfig=pam.figure(association_matrices['correlation'],'7a · '+CORRELATION.capitalize()+' association · '+matrix_scope,cp.LABELS,FACTOR_LABELS,signed=True)\ndisplay_figure(fig);plt.close(fig)\nif SHOW_MATRIX_DETAILS:\n    display(correlation_details[['child_state','factor','predictor','correlation','n_finite','n_total','finite_pct']])\n    display(association_matrices['correlation_counts'])\n"),
        nbformat.v4.new_markdown_cell("### 7b. Relative magnitude: eight controls × ten predictors\n\nEach cell is the **main-effect contribution to that predictor's own grid variance**. This measures how much predictor variation is attributable to changing a control after averaging over the other controls. It is not a signed change, an absolute physical magnitude, or predictive performance.\n\nFor example, a strong correlation can coexist with a small variance contribution. Interactions are not included in this matrix; they appear next. Gray indicates a constant predictor or incomplete full-grid values. We do not impute undefined ratios or recompute factorial shares on a truncated grid.\n\nColours use a linear, figure-specific scale and cells print the percentages. Compare numbers rather than colours across different matrices. In overall mode, contributions are averaged equally across types with defined predictor variance; available-type counts are shown in optional details. This may differ from the response-resolved population in Section 6."),
        nbformat.v4.new_code_cell("fig=pam.figure(association_matrices['main'],'7b · Main-effect contribution to predictor variance · '+matrix_scope,cp.LABELS,FACTOR_LABELS)\ndisplay_figure(fig);plt.close(fig)\nif SHOW_MATRIX_DETAILS:display(association_matrices['main_counts'])\n"),
        nbformat.v4.new_markdown_cell("### 7c. Combined effects: 28 control interactions × ten predictors\n\nThese percentages measure the **additional pair effect after removing both main effects**. They are not correlations with a raw product such as length × width. A nonzero entry means the predictor represents that combination beyond the individual effects; compare the same pair with Section 6.\n\nAll 28 pairs are shown, without selecting only the strongest. Main effects, pair interactions and the remaining higher-order interactions together account for each resolved predictor's variance. Therefore these pair entries alone need not sum to 100%. Similarity to the response pattern supports mechanistic relevance but does not establish correct prediction, direction or amplitude."),
        nbformat.v4.new_code_cell("fig=pam.figure(association_matrices['interaction'],'7c · Pair-interaction contribution to predictor variance · '+matrix_scope,cp.LABELS,FACTOR_LABELS)\ndisplay_figure(fig);plt.close(fig)\nif SHOW_MATRIX_DETAILS:display(association_matrices['interaction_counts'])\n"),
        nbformat.v4.new_markdown_cell('### 7d. Compare differences with the existing spread and entropy ratios\n\nThe ten original predictors in 7a–7c are unchanged. Here we test alternatives alongside the ratios, using the **same control-association and variance-contribution matrices**. No fitted-model comparison or replacement decision is made.\n\nFor route probabilities $w_p$ and complete route lengths $L_p$ in the B comparison domain,\n$\\bar L=\\sum_p w_p L_p$, $\\sigma_L=\\sqrt{\\sum_p w_p(L_p-\\bar L)^2}$ and $H=-\\sum_p w_p\\log w_p$.\n\n| Quantity | Existing predictor | Candidate difference |\n|---|---|---|\n| Route-length spread | $\\log(1+\\sigma_{L,c}/\\sigma_{L,p})$ | $\\sigma_{L,c}-\\sigma_{L,p}$, in metres |\n| Route-length spread, dimensionless | Same existing ratio | $(\\sigma_{L,c}-\\sigma_{L,p})/\\bar L_p$ |\n| Route entropy | $\\log(1+H_c/H_p)$ | $H_c-H_p$ |\n\nDifferences use **child minus parent**. The normalized spread difference divides by positive parent mean route length, not by potentially zero parent spread. Entropy difference is already dimensionless.\n\nA full collapse has zero child spread and entropy. Existing ratios therefore become zero regardless of how much was removed (or undefined if parent spread is zero). Differences retain the amount removed. With identical route lengths, spread difference correctly equals zero: the feature definition is valid, even though those cases have no geometric length spread to remove.\n\nAbsolute spread difference retains network scale. The normalized difference removes uniform length scaling. This is a deliberate trade-off to inspect, not a reason to automatically prefer either one. These are route-length descriptors, not direct predictions of hydrograph spread.\n\nAll new candidates are computed from the same geometry/domain implementation as the existing predictors. Their values do not depend on the depth method; their relation to a response can still differ between methods. Gray/undefined factorial cells remain distinct from valid zero effects.'),
        nbformat.v4.new_code_cell("import spread_entropy_comparison as se\ncomparison_provenance=json.loads((se.OUT/'provenance.json').read_text())\nfor path,digest in comparison_provenance['source_sha256'].items():\n    assert hashlib.sha256(Path(path).read_bytes()).hexdigest()==digest, f'Changed comparison source: {path}'\ncomparison_features=pd.read_parquet(se.OUT/'features.parquet')\ncomparison_effects=pd.concat([mechanisms,pd.read_csv(se.OUT/'factorial_signatures.csv')],ignore_index=True)\ncomparison_matrices,comparison_correlations=pam.matrices(comparison_features,comparison_effects,manifest['config']['grid'],se.COMPARE,CONTROL_METHOD,matrix_treatment,CORRELATION)\nnew_coverage=pd.read_csv(se.OUT/'coverage.csv')\ncomparison_coverage=pd.concat([coverage,new_coverage],ignore_index=True)\ncc=comparison_coverage[comparison_coverage.method.eq(CONTROL_METHOD)&comparison_coverage.variable.isin(se.COMPARE)]\nif matrix_treatment:cc=cc[cc.child_state.eq(matrix_treatment)]\ndisplay(cc[['child_state','variable','n','finite','complete','resolved']])\nfor name,title,signed in [('correlation','Association with controls',True),('main','Main-effect variance contributions',False),('interaction','All 28 pair-interaction variance contributions',False)]:\n    fig=pam.figure(comparison_matrices[name],'7d · '+title+' · '+matrix_scope,se.LABELS,FACTOR_LABELS,signed=signed)\n    display_figure(fig);plt.close(fig)\n"),
        nbformat.v4.new_markdown_cell("### 7e. Main synthetic story — two figures\n\n**Network collapse changes timing by changing the travel paths experienced by discharge.** Under the tested Manning setup, branch length is the strongest control: it changes travel distance and, at fixed head loss, slope. Network scale and allocation determine the size of the response, including important interactions. Centroid and peak share these mechanisms, but peak timing is more sensitive to slope-related effects.\n\n**The predictors describe different parts of that mechanism.** Relative resistance and length ratios capture relative path/allocation changes but lose absolute scale. Domain size and mean route-length change provide scale; width dominance and entropy describe allocation/diversity; slope supplies hydraulic context. Inverse-depth ΔG combines several mechanisms and resembles the centroid sensitivity pattern. The new spread and entropy differences retain information discarded by full-collapse ratios; their added predictive value remains untested.\n\nRead the two figures together:\n1. **What changes the response?** Main controls and the four strongest response pair interactions, shown as disjoint variance contributions. Higher-order and other pair contributions remain in the supporting analyses.\n2. **Which predictors represent those changes?** The same controls/interactions against all ten current predictors and the three new difference candidates. Colours and printed values describe each predictor's own variance, not predictive performance or signed effect.\n\nBoth figures use the same response-resolved treatment population and equal type weighting within the selected hydraulic method. Predictor labels show available-type counts: undefined or constant predictor columns cannot contribute variance shares. The main-island near-zero response is excluded by the existing resolution screen. These are conditional findings for the tested grid, forcing, width-based allocation and hydraulic closure. Details in Sections 6–7d support these two summary figures."),
        nbformat.v4.new_code_cell("from synthetic_story import figures as synthetic_story_figures\nSTORY_METHOD='variable_manning'  # main synthesis; other depth methods are sensitivity comparisons\nstory_labels={**cp.LABELS,**se.LABELS,'length_delta_km':'Mean route-length change (km)'}\nstory_figures,story_info=synthetic_story_figures(comparison_effects,coverage,STORY_METHOD,CURRENT_PREDICTORS+se.COLUMNS,story_labels)\nprint('Summary method:',STORY_METHOD,'; contributing response types:',story_info['types'])\nfor number,fig in enumerate(story_figures,1):\n    display_figure(fig)\n    name='synthetic_story_'+str(number)+'_'+STORY_METHOD\n    fig.savefig(se.OUT/(name+'.png'),dpi=170,bbox_inches='tight')\n    fig.savefig(se.OUT/(name+'.pdf'),bbox_inches='tight')\n    plt.close(fig)\n")
    ]
    for c in cells:c.metadata['tags']=[TAG]
    nb.cells.extend(cells);nbformat.write(nb,p)
if __name__=='__main__':install()
